00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:56.340
The first question
is about eye health,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.070
in particular eye
health protocols

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.100
for people that are aging
but eye health protocols

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.780
for people in general.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.630
The question is, I'm
noticing that my vision,

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.570
both close and far,
is deteriorating.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.930
What are the best eye
exercises, eye health behaviors,

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.510
supplements, et cetera
for the aging eye?

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.280
So the answer I'm
about to provide

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.870
applies to everybody
regardless of age,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:23.910
which is, you want to make sure
that you view things both close

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:27.780
up and far away across your day.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.990
Many, many people nowadays
spend a lot of time

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.840
looking at their smartphone,
looking at the computer screen,

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.160
looking at books and
paper, in other words,

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.640
looking at things somewhere
between 9 inches away

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.050
from their face and their
eyes, maybe 2 and 1/2 feet away

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.190
from their face and eyes.

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:48.520
This is an unprecedented
event in human history,

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.850
as far as we know,
because it wasn't

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.640
until the advent
of the smartphone

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.970
that we've spent so much
time looking at screens

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.640
at close range
relative to looking

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.940
at things at further
distances from our face

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.390
throughout the day.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.090
In fact, it should
come as no surprise

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.750
that the incidence of
myopia, of nearsightedness,

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:13.020
is increasing dramatically
around the world

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.710
but is increasing
particularly fast in children

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.990
and in young adults
and even in adults

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:22.320
who are viewing things at very
close range, so 2 and 1/2 feet

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.100
or less.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.050
Now, I am not saying
that viewing things

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.080
at 2 and 1/2 feet or less is bad
for your eyes, that is simply

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:28.960
not the case.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.220
However, if you want your
vision to be maintained or even

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.800
improve, it's going
to be very important

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.020
for you to view
things at a distance

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.718
as well for ideally
several hours per day.

00:03:40.718 --> 00:03:43.260
Now, I realize some people just
simply won't accomplish this.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.390
In fact, I'd like everyone
to just take a moment

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.850
and do a rough
estimation in your head

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:51.270
of how much time during
your daytime activities

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:54.540
you are viewing things at,
say, 2 and 1/2 feet or 3

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.990
feet or less, so
smartphone, computer screen,

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:01.650
paper, books, et cetera,
versus viewing things

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.780
that are 4 feet
away, 8 feet away,

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.830
so maybe conversation across the
table with another human being.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:08.963
Remember those?

00:04:08.963 --> 00:04:10.380
Some of us are
doing that more now

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:15.460
that we seem to be emerging
from this whole pandemic phase.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:18.089
However, many people are
still mostly just viewing

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:20.162
things within very close range.

00:04:20.162 --> 00:04:22.620
And, in fact, if you were to
ask yourself, how much time do

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.100
you spend looking at things at
the distance of many hundreds

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.340
of feet, or taking a walk
where you're not looking

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.410
at any screen and
you're simply letting

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.390
visual images or
the visual imagery

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.720
rather pass by on your
eyes, I think for most of us

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.150
that number is getting
progressively smaller

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.650
and smaller with
each passing year.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:45.480
So this is an issue because
the structure of the eye

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.240
is such that the lens
of the eye can move,

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.350
but also that the length of
the eyeball from front to back

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:55.530
is actually impacted
by how close

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.320
or how far you
happen to view things

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.158
during the course of your day,
especially during development.

00:05:01.158 --> 00:05:02.700
There's a classic
experiment that I'd

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.220
like to just briefly
describe to you which

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.380
has been performed in chickens.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.060
It's been performed in mice.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.280
It's also been
performed on humans,

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:16.900
where a visual occluder
or a visual image.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:21.510
So an occluder is just a black
screen or a piece of cardboard

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:27.000
or an image, maybe some black
and white checks or checkers,

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.000
are placed at a certain
distance from the eye,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.240
either up close or far
away, and the impact

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.210
of that on the
length of the eyeball

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:40.290
and on the structure of the lens
and on the quality and acuity

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.120
of vision is then measured.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:45.450
And to make a long story short,
what these studies show is

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:49.890
that, if during development,
you or an animal

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:52.770
exclusively looks at things
that are up close, very

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:56.160
close to the eye, and doesn't
ever get long-range vision,

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.140
the eyeball lengthens.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.360
And when that happens the
lens, which, of course, is

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.777
in the front of
the eyeball, which

00:06:01.777 --> 00:06:03.940
focuses the light onto
the back of the eyeball,

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:05.760
which is where the
so-called neural retina

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.790
is, where the cells
that sense light are,

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:13.950
that visual image
lands in front of as

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:18.000
opposed to directly on
the light-sensing portion

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.000
of the eyeball that we
call the neural retina.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:26.430
It lands too close or near
the lens as opposed to

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:27.593
on the back of the eyeball.

00:06:27.593 --> 00:06:29.010
And that's part
of the reason what

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:33.060
we call the consequence of
that nearsighted myopia.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.500
In far sightedness,
the opposite happens.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.775
The visual image is actually
focused behind the retina.

00:06:38.775 --> 00:06:41.400
It doesn't land directly on the
retina, which is what you want.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.860
It's focused too far
behind the retina.

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:47.280
And that's due to a
shortening of the eyeball.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:48.660
So what I just
described actually

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.720
explains pretty well why kids
who look at computer screens,

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:56.190
or books up close
often need glasses.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:59.573
When I was growing up, they
called this nerd syndrome.

00:06:59.573 --> 00:07:01.740
Raising my hand for those
of you that are listening.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:05.580
I read an awful lot, but I also
spend time outside fortunately

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.218
and running around
on the soccer field

00:07:07.218 --> 00:07:08.760
and looking at things
in the distance

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:11.250
and playing in the
neighborhood with my friends.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:15.150
Kids that spend the vast
amount of their time looking

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:16.650
at things up close,
and particularly

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:20.520
children who do that indoors
for most of their waking time,

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.235
well, those kids develop myopia.

00:07:22.235 --> 00:07:23.610
This is part of
the reason why we

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:28.973
have such an increase of the
incidence of myopia nowadays.

00:07:28.973 --> 00:07:31.390
What's interesting, and I think
most people don't realize,

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:34.390
is that even as a young adult
and adult, and perhaps even as

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:37.210
an older adult,
an elderly person,

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:40.660
looking at things up close to
much at the expense of getting

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:43.030
some time viewing
things at a distance,

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:46.150
in particular outdoors, can
be very problematic because it

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:47.440
can exacerbate myopia.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:48.940
So the short,
direct prescription

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:53.320
here is to make sure that you're
getting at least an hour or so

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.680
each day, although ideally it
would be more, of long viewing.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.020
And that could be done by
walking outside and avoiding

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:00.880
looking at your
phone while walking.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.550
It doesn't require that you
look at a particular object

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:06.310
in the distance, although if
you are scanning for and looking

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:10.810
for objects in the distance,
the way that you might on a hike

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:13.660
or you're looking out
toward or off a vista

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:15.670
or you're looking for
someone in the distance,

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:18.040
all of that sort of
visual scanning behavior

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.260
is going to be very
good for your eye health

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:22.570
and for maintaining
vision at a distance.

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:24.550
If you're somebody
who's far sighted

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:26.650
and you have trouble
seeing things up close,

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:28.270
well, then you need to ask
yourself whether or not

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:29.950
you're spending too much
time looking at things off

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:31.780
in the distance, although
I want to be very

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:34.419
clear that nearsightedness
and farsightedness aren't

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:37.990
necessarily the consequence
of this near, far viewing.

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:40.240
There can be independent
causes of nearsightedness

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:41.260
and farsightedness.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:43.463
But for many people
out there who

00:08:43.463 --> 00:08:45.880
are spending progressively
more and more time just looking

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:49.570
at things up close, you
can get this lengthening

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:52.780
of the eyeball or
challenges in the way

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:55.450
that the elasticity of
the lens is controlled

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:56.980
by the musculature
in ways that make

00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:01.480
it hard to maintain sharp,
crisp vision when you're

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:03.800
looking at things at a
distance or up close.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.880
So I can't, because there
are simply no studies

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:10.660
to support the idea that you
need exactly two hours per day

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:13.450
of outside long vision at a
particular distance and/or

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:16.147
that you want to cut off
or have a threshold of four

00:09:16.147 --> 00:09:17.980
hours per day of looking
at things up close,

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:19.960
but we can say with a
high degree of confidence

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:22.180
that if you want to
maintain or enhance

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:25.750
your vision at all distances,
that for every hour or so

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:28.720
that you spend
looking at things,

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.750
say, a distance of
3 feet away or less,

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:35.230
that you want to get at least
one hour per day total, doesn't

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:36.250
have to be all at once,

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:38.050
of looking at things
out in the distance

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:42.700
or looking at things beyond 3
feet relative to the distance

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.120
from your eye.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:48.430
So I think for some people
who are already badly myopic,

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:50.455
this isn't going to
remedy that myopia.

00:09:50.455 --> 00:09:52.330
You're still going to
need corrective lenses,

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:54.460
but if you're somebody,
similar to the person who

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:58.480
asks the question, that they're
quote unquote "noticing"

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.940
their vision is
deteriorating, really

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:04.030
try and balance out
the amount of viewing

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:06.460
that you're doing at close
distance and long distance.

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:09.820
And, again, I would not obsess
about the total numbers.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:12.550
It doesn't have to be three
hours and three hours,

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:14.860
but the more time that you
can spend at viewing things

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:17.140
that variable distance
throughout the day,

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:19.840
the better off you're going to
be in maintaining and perhaps

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:21.610
even enhancing your
vision somewhat.

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:23.530
Now, there's an
additional behavioral tool

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:26.110
that we can glean from the
recent scientific literature

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:29.710
exploring myopia which
points to the fact

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:33.820
that children who get
two hours a day or more

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:37.600
of time outside in sunlight--

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:38.840
one of my favorite topics.

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:41.380
I know many people are
probably rolling their eyes

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:44.050
right now when they
hear me say you really

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:45.467
want to view morning
sunlight, not

00:10:45.467 --> 00:10:46.758
through a window or windshield.

00:10:46.758 --> 00:10:49.422
Don't wear sunglasses for that,
trying to get as much sunlight

00:10:49.422 --> 00:10:51.130
as you can in your
eyes early in the day.

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:52.840
Never stare at any light
so bright it's painful,

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:53.830
look at, et cetera.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.390
We've done a lot of
episodes on this.

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:57.063
We have a Light
for Health episode,

00:10:57.063 --> 00:10:58.480
this Isn't All the
Sleep episodes.

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:00.220
It's just such a
basic foundation

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.805
of circadian rhythm and health
that I just keep repeating it.

00:11:02.805 --> 00:11:04.930
However, there are also
large-scale clinical trials

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.000
involving thousands
of subjects that

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.000
have shown that people,
children in particular, who

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:13.810
get two hours or more of
outside time every day

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.180
have a much lower
incidence of myopia.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:19.000
Now, what's really interesting
about these studies is that,

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:22.960
while some of those kids are
looking at things further

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:26.170
off in the distance than they
would if they were indoors

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:29.480
on their tablets or screens
or with their nose in a book,

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:31.300
it does not seem to
be the case that they

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.133
have to be looking at
things in the distance

00:11:33.133 --> 00:11:36.430
in order to see this
reduced incidence of myopia.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:38.530
What's probably
happening instead

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:40.540
is that the
particular wavelengths

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:42.730
of light that
emanate from the sun,

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.280
that photon energy in
particular is triggering

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:47.170
the activation of the
so-called intrinsically

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:49.570
sensitive melanopsin and retinal
ganglion cells, which we know

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:51.903
are important for setting
circadian rhythm for enhancing

00:11:51.903 --> 00:11:53.650
mood focus and
alertness during the day

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:57.310
and enhancing the quality and
duration of sleep at night.

00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:59.590
But those melanopsin and
intrinsically photosensitive

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:03.370
ganglion cells are also
involved in networks

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:06.160
within the eye that
relate to blood flow, that

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:09.820
relate to the
ciliary body, which

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:13.930
is a structure within the eye
that controls the aperture

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:15.790
and movement of
some key components

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:20.440
within the eye, that also relate
to the crispness of vision,

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:23.663
that is the acuity of vision,
at both short and long range.

00:12:23.663 --> 00:12:25.580
So there are a lot of
technical details there.

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:29.680
In fact, I really hope to
get the great Russell Van

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:31.303
Gelder, or Dr.
Russell Van Gelder,

00:12:31.303 --> 00:12:33.970
who's the chair of ophthalmology
at the University of Washington

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:37.210
in Seattle, who is a
world expert in this

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:40.750
and the various aspects,
both molecular and structural

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:42.610
of the ciliary
body and the lens,

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:45.760
and he's an expert in uveitis,
a very interesting topic

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:47.828
in its own, to come
onto the podcast

00:12:47.828 --> 00:12:49.120
and talk more about eye health.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:51.203
And we will do that in the
not too distant future.

00:12:51.203 --> 00:12:54.173
Meanwhile, if the first
behavioral protocol

00:12:54.173 --> 00:12:55.840
for enhancing and
maintaining eye health

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.570
is to make sure that you're
spending at least some time

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:02.530
balancing out the near vision
and far vision during the day,

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:04.120
it also stands to
reason that you

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:06.800
want to get outside for
at least two hours a day,

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:10.300
which I know sounds like a lot,
in order to prevent myopia,

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:13.420
if you're a young person,
and offset the progression of

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:14.878
and maybe even
reverse some myopia,

00:13:14.878 --> 00:13:16.378
we don't know yet
based on the data,

00:13:16.378 --> 00:13:17.800
but there's some
indication that's

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:20.200
possible, by getting
outside two hours a day.

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:23.597
And the cool thing is, because
many people, including myself,

00:13:23.597 --> 00:13:25.930
have a lot of work to do and
we need to be on our phones

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:28.347
quite a lot, or we choose to
be on our phones quite a lot,

00:13:28.347 --> 00:13:31.600
this effect of getting sunlight
outdoors during the daytime

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.200
for two hours or
more does not seem

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:38.553
to require that you stay
off your phone or tablet,

00:13:38.553 --> 00:13:40.720
or that you're looking at
things far in the distance

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:41.660
the entire time.

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:43.600
What this means
is, if you can get

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:48.280
outside onto a patio or a deck
or an outdoor table if you can,

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:50.170
if weather and
conditions permit,

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:54.100
and do some of your phone time
and computer time and reading,

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:55.025
et cetera, outdoors.

00:13:55.025 --> 00:13:56.650
Now, if you're already
making an effort

00:13:56.650 --> 00:13:59.440
to get outside, hike,
play sports, take walks,

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:02.080
et cetera, that of course
counts towards this two hour

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:02.697
threshold.

00:14:02.697 --> 00:14:04.780
And I know this sounds
like a lot, this two hours,

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:09.227
and yet for most people,
it certainly will vary,

00:14:09.227 --> 00:14:10.810
depending on time
of year, but I think

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:14.080
striving to get on average
about two hours of outdoor time

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:16.750
as much as you can or
opening a window, which

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:19.390
wouldn't be as good as getting
outside, but opening a window

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:21.370
and trying to get some natural
light exposure to the eye

00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:23.020
to trigger the activity,
these intrinsically

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:25.600
photosensitive melanopsin and
retinal ganglion cells, that's

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:29.080
going to be a really good idea
in trying to offset and maybe

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:30.772
even reverse some vision loss.

00:14:30.772 --> 00:14:32.230
Now, the other
behavioral protocols

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:35.590
which have shown to be useful
in terms of maintaining

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:39.490
or improving vision relate to
the musculature around the eye

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:41.950
and the control of the
movement of the lens.

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:44.930
And actually, the lens of the
eye has its own flexibility.

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:46.960
It's a really, really
interesting structure.

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:48.865
I'll geek out on
this far too long

00:14:48.865 --> 00:14:50.780
if I don't prevent
myself from doing so,

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:52.390
but the lens is just incredible.

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:55.990
The fact that it has a
specific structure of proteins

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:58.330
like crystallines and
things of that sort

00:14:58.330 --> 00:15:03.340
and that it can still derive
nourishment from blood flow

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:05.630
but does not actually have
vasculature within it.

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:07.855
So it's not-- if you notice
the lens of your eyes

00:15:07.855 --> 00:15:09.730
and filled with blood
vessels and capillaries

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:11.800
because it needs
light to pass through,

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:14.470
super interesting
structure I think

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:18.430
the only violation of what
I just said is the manatee.

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:21.100
Those pigs are underwater,
elephant-like creatures

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:23.725
that I think were
or are endangered.

00:15:23.725 --> 00:15:25.600
Down in Florida, they
were getting chopped up

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:27.183
by speedboats, but
I think now there's

00:15:27.183 --> 00:15:29.530
some effort to preserve
those big gentle giants.

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:33.940
The manatee, as I recall, has
a vascularized lens, which

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:35.680
is why their eyes look opaque.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:38.020
It looks like they
have cataracts.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:41.020
In any case, you're
not a manatee.

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:44.890
You have no blood vessel
supply to the lens.

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:47.170
Keeping that lens clear
is vital nowadays.

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:48.880
They can do lens replacement.

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:52.315
There's really a pretty
incredible progress

00:15:52.315 --> 00:15:55.930
in the realm of ophthalmology
and surgical replacement

00:15:55.930 --> 00:15:59.020
of the lens or the cornea,
other aspects of the eye.

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:02.230
But if you're somebody who
is interested in maintaining

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:03.760
your vision, you're
going to want

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:05.920
to maintain the
musculature that controls

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:08.380
the movement and the change
of shape in that lens

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:12.340
and the movement of the eyes,
which allows you to transition

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:14.830
your vision from very
sharp when viewing things

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:17.920
up close to very sharp one
viewing things at a distance.

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:21.850
And this is where some of the
so-called near-far exercises

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:22.940
can be very useful.

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:25.540
So when we did a full
length podcast on eye health

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:27.490
and improving eyesight,
which I invite

00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:29.080
you to listen to
if you like, talked

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:32.740
about two different types
of vision exercises that

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:35.530
have been shown to be
useful for improving vision

00:16:35.530 --> 00:16:37.840
at a level of acuity
that have a lot

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:41.410
to do with improving the
musculature and the movement

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:42.760
of the eyes and the lens.

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:44.770
And that's what's called
a smooth pursuit task.

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:48.040
So you could go on to YouTube if
you like and put smooth pursuit

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:50.890
eye task and do this for
a few minutes each day

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:52.840
if you're trying to
keep your vision strong.

00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:55.600
And what that task involves
is, as the name suggests,

00:16:55.600 --> 00:17:00.580
smoothly tracking a small
dot or crosshatch or arrow

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:02.230
on the screen, which
is very different

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:04.510
than the so-called
saccade type eye movements

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:05.349
that we often make.

00:17:05.349 --> 00:17:08.079
Saccades are when you dart your
eyes to a particular location

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:10.000
to look at something that
dart it to another location

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:10.875
to look at something.

00:17:10.875 --> 00:17:12.670
Microsaccades are
little, tiny versions

00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:15.260
of those that were doing
most all of the time,

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:16.190
believe it or not.

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:18.819
When you look at a
visual scene or image,

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:21.430
if you did not have those
little microsaccades,

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:24.099
your brain would adapt
or would attenuate

00:17:24.099 --> 00:17:26.447
to whatever you were looking
at, and the visual image

00:17:26.447 --> 00:17:28.030
would actually
disappear, which really

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:30.880
speaks to how important it is
that your perceptions change

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:32.080
in order to maintain them.

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:34.030
This is very similar
to where if something

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:36.760
is placed on your
hand, say a finger,

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:38.650
you'll notice that
change, but then

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:40.810
if you leave it there
for a short while,

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:43.330
you'll forget that it's
there because the cells there

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:44.050
have adapted out.

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:44.750
There's no change.

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:46.660
So your nervous system is
largely responding to change.

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:48.410
This is also why when
you walk into a room

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:50.440
and you'll often
notice a novel smell,

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:52.660
let's hope it's a good
smell, after a few minutes

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:54.400
or so, you don't notice
the smell anymore

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:56.440
because your olfactory
system adapted that away.

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:58.240
So a lot of that
adaptation is prevented

00:17:58.240 --> 00:17:59.800
by those little microsaccades.

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:02.590
And the macrosaccades,
the bigger saccades,

00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:04.360
are really for moving
your eyes around

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:05.860
to look for things
in visual space.

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:08.320
Smooth pursuit is a
very particular kind

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:10.960
of visual behavior that you
can do in a smooth pursuit

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:12.310
task and zero cost.

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:15.700
Costs a little bit of time I
suppose to do this on YouTube.

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:17.020
And most any of them will do.

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:19.270
We can provide a link in the
show note captions of one

00:18:19.270 --> 00:18:21.190
that we like or
several that we like,

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:24.560
but by doing that smooth
pursuit task for a bit each day,

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:26.110
maybe just a minute
or two, you're

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:29.830
maintaining your ability through
the musculature of the eye

00:18:29.830 --> 00:18:31.270
to do smooth pursuit.

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:33.190
And, of course, you
do smooth pursuit

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:35.110
when you move about
your day, but for people

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:37.600
that are trying to offset or
reverse vision loss, doing

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:39.820
some additional deliberate
smooth pursuit exercises

00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:40.670
can be useful.

00:18:40.670 --> 00:18:43.510
The other visual exercise
that can be very useful

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:45.500
is the so-called
near-far exercise.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:48.370
So this is best
accomplished, I think,

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:53.440
by holding out a pen or
pencil in front of your eyes,

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:55.537
in front of your head, and
focusing very intensely

00:18:55.537 --> 00:18:58.120
through what's called a vergence
eye movement, where you bring

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:01.120
both eyes to the tip of
the pen and then moving

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:05.110
that pen closer and closer
and closer to your eyes,

00:19:05.110 --> 00:19:08.410
where you get close
enough that you actually

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:11.092
have to deliberately
bring your eyes--

00:19:11.092 --> 00:19:12.550
for those of you
listening, this is

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:14.925
what I'm doing right now-- to
the point where it's blurry

00:19:14.925 --> 00:19:18.310
and you can no longer hold
it in sharp, crisp relief.

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:19.685
You can't see it
sharply that is.

00:19:19.685 --> 00:19:21.768
And then you can move it
out a little bit distance

00:19:21.768 --> 00:19:23.320
and play right
around that threshold

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:27.550
where it's hard to
keep it in focus

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:28.900
and then move it back out again.

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:31.930
And then in doing that for even
a minute or two, you'll find

00:19:31.930 --> 00:19:33.580
is a bit of a
strain on your eyes,

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:35.560
but you can actually
build up the musculature

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:37.690
and the neuromuscular
connections that allow you

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:38.860
to do that more efficiently.

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:40.660
And that has been
shown to improve

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:42.730
certain aspects of vision,
especially when people

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:44.560
are diligent about
doing those exercises,

00:19:44.560 --> 00:19:46.240
not necessarily every
day, but let's say

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:47.380
three days a week or so.

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:48.297
They can be very fast.

00:19:48.297 --> 00:19:49.870
I actually find
them fun for reasons

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:51.683
that escape me entirely.

00:19:51.683 --> 00:19:53.350
Now, how close you
can bring that object

00:19:53.350 --> 00:19:55.270
and still maintain
it in sharp focus

00:19:55.270 --> 00:19:59.060
will vary from person to person
based on a number of things.

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:02.560
First of all, how healthy
and well-performing

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:04.330
their neural retina
is, whether or not

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:08.680
they have myopia or hyperopia,
nearsightedness, et cetera,

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:11.920
whether or not, for
instance, you're

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:14.915
like me and you have a fairly
close inter pupillary distance,

00:20:14.915 --> 00:20:16.540
or whether or not
you're more wall eyed

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:18.248
and you have your eyes
set further apart.

00:20:18.248 --> 00:20:20.860
All those things will
dictate the exact performance

00:20:20.860 --> 00:20:22.810
of that task, but
nonetheless, it's

00:20:22.810 --> 00:20:25.670
a very useful task to do when
trying to maintain or improve

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:26.170
vision.

00:20:26.170 --> 00:20:28.378
Now, of course, there are
a lot more behavioral tools

00:20:28.378 --> 00:20:30.490
that one could use to
maintain or enhance vision.

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:32.630
I'm really touching
on the biggest

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:34.630
and what I consider the
most important ones that

00:20:34.630 --> 00:20:36.547
are most accessible to
people without the need

00:20:36.547 --> 00:20:38.870
for any fancy equipment at all.

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:41.822
And if you want to learn more
about other behavioral tools

00:20:41.822 --> 00:20:43.780
that one can use to
maintain or enhance vision,

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:45.820
please see the episode
that we did on eyesight.

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:47.410
You can simply go
to hubermanlab.com.

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:49.775
Put into the search
function, eyesight or vision.

00:20:49.775 --> 00:20:50.900
It'll take you right to it.

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:53.150
In fact, everything on that
website is keyword indexed

00:20:53.150 --> 00:20:56.350
and will take you to the
various episodes and time stamps

00:20:56.350 --> 00:21:00.550
that you're interested in
according to that keyword.

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:03.050
Now, the person who asked
this question, who by the way,

00:21:03.050 --> 00:21:05.020
is Page Singer, thank
you Page Singer,

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:06.640
and the question
got a lot of upvotes

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:09.370
because apparently there's
a lot of interest in this,

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:12.820
understandably so, asked about
nutrition and supplements

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:15.850
for maintaining or
improving vision.

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:17.620
That could be a very
lengthy conversation,

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:19.900
but let's just summarize
it with a few brief points

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:23.500
that we know stand based on
the clinical and scientific

00:21:23.500 --> 00:21:24.773
research.

00:21:24.773 --> 00:21:26.940
We've all heard that carrots
are good for your eyes.

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.430
Well, it's not carrots per se
that are good for your eyes.

00:21:29.430 --> 00:21:32.250
It's vitamin A that's
essential for the so-called

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:33.630
phototransduction cascade.

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:37.073
The phototransduction
cascade is the process

00:21:37.073 --> 00:21:38.490
by which the
photoreceptors, which

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:41.040
are in the back of the retina,
at least if you're human,

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:42.750
they're in the
back of the retina,

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:44.910
take photons, light
energy, and convert it

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:46.500
into electrical
signals that's then

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:49.800
conveyed to the other cells
and neurons within the retina

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:52.980
and then passed into the brain
to create visual perceps.

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:56.130
Most people can't get enough
of this fat soluble vitamin,

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:58.590
we call vitamin
A, by making sure

00:21:58.590 --> 00:22:02.040
that they eat at least some
dark green leafy vegetables.

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:05.730
And that they do
that in their closest

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:07.830
to raw form, maybe
cooked a little bit

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:09.092
but not overly cooked.

00:22:09.092 --> 00:22:10.800
There are other sources
of vitamin A out.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:13.590
There you can look those
up online just for sources

00:22:13.590 --> 00:22:16.260
of vitamin A. Most people will
be able to get enough vitamin

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:19.470
A from their nutrition and won't
require it from a supplement,

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:23.460
provided that it's not present
at excessively high levels

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:26.550
in a foundational supplement
or vitamin mineral supplement.

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:28.230
Getting some
additional vitamin A

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:31.650
from a foundational supplement
or vitamin mineral supplement

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:34.110
is probably not going to be
an issue unless you really

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:36.360
take in far too
much of that vitamin

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:38.580
A. It could potentially
provide some insurance.

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:40.830
But, again, I think most
people can get enough vitamin

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:43.150
A from their diet if
they are taking carrots

00:22:43.150 --> 00:22:45.480
or to eat the foods
that contain vitamin A,

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:47.250
green leafy
vegetables being one.

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:49.890
The other food that's known to
be highly enriched in vitamin A

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:53.190
is liver, one of my least
favorite foods regardless

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:54.960
of what animal that
liver comes from.

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:59.310
Liver is somewhat of a
popular/controversial organ

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:01.740
meat in the nutrition
space for reasons

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:04.050
that aren't interesting,
for sake of this discussion

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:05.903
anyway I suppose
for those of you

00:23:05.903 --> 00:23:08.070
that like liver, you could
eat a little bit of liver

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:08.987
every once in a while.

00:23:08.987 --> 00:23:11.843
It is rich in a number of things
that are beneficial to health,

00:23:11.843 --> 00:23:13.260
although for you
vegans out there,

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:15.312
I'm sure you'll want to
avoid liver entirely.

00:23:15.312 --> 00:23:16.770
I avoid liver
because I just simply

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:18.030
don't like the way it tastes.

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:19.590
Maybe once or twice
I've tasted liver

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:20.860
that was appetizing to me.

00:23:20.860 --> 00:23:24.330
And it can be OK, but it's just
not, or at least by my read it,

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:27.300
can be OK, but it's not
a food that I actively

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:30.648
seek out, order, or prepare
for myself on a regular basis.

00:23:30.648 --> 00:23:33.190
So fortunately, there are other
sources of vitamin A as well.

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:37.800
And, again, I just invite you to
all put into a web search food

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:39.810
sources of vitamin
and figure out

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:42.030
how to get your daily
dose of vitamin A

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:44.490
by taking in foods
that you like for you.

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:46.590
And then in terms
of supplementation

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:50.170
of things that can enhance
vision, this is a newer area.

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:52.620
And frankly, it's an area
where most of the data

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:56.340
point to some supplements
that can potentially

00:23:56.340 --> 00:23:59.410
be beneficial in certain
disease conditions.

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:03.450
So for instance, in fairly
progressed age-related macular

00:24:03.450 --> 00:24:04.320
degeneration.

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:06.150
Age-related macular
degeneration is

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:09.690
fairly common, or more
common than we would like,

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:11.130
we should say.

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:13.500
And it does appear
that supplementing

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:17.850
with certain things like lutein,
which is present in eggs,

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:20.220
or egg yolks in particular,
especially egg yolks that

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:23.610
aren't overly cooked, or
that can be supplemented

00:24:23.610 --> 00:24:27.840
can potentially help
with some instances

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:30.120
of age-related
macular degeneration.

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:31.830
But the data on this
are still emerging.

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:33.622
I consulted with our
chair of ophthalmology

00:24:33.622 --> 00:24:36.090
at Stanford about lutein
and some of the other things

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:39.300
that you'll see out there, like
zeaxanthin and astaxanthins.

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:44.363
And these xanthins come
from certain fruits

00:24:44.363 --> 00:24:46.530
like blueberries, and they're
present in supplements

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:47.550
and things of that sort.

00:24:47.550 --> 00:24:50.540
The consensus I
got talking to him

00:24:50.540 --> 00:24:52.290
and talking to some
other ophthalmologists

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:55.470
are that if you're really
aiming to get a healthy diet,

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:57.750
you're avoiding
cigarette smoking.

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:01.140
And by healthy
diet I mean getting

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:03.120
sufficient amounts of
vitamin A and lutein

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:04.170
through food sources.

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:06.990
Again, you can look up where
those food sources are,

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:09.215
egg yolks just
being one of them.

00:25:09.215 --> 00:25:10.590
Well, then chances
are you're not

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:14.430
going to need to supplement
with lutein or the zeaxanthins

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:15.520
or the astaxanthins.

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:19.740
However, I have heard anecdotal
reports from a number of people

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:22.217
that they'll start taking a
supplement for eye health,

00:25:22.217 --> 00:25:23.800
there are a number
of them out there--

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:25.380
we are not, as a
podcast, affiliate

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:27.780
with any of those eye health
supplements by the way--

00:25:27.780 --> 00:25:31.620
and people are reporting
enhanced vision.

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:33.690
I receive emails all
the time that say, I'm

00:25:33.690 --> 00:25:36.780
taking this lutein at
20 milligrams per day,

00:25:36.780 --> 00:25:39.300
and there I should mention
that the dosage that's

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:40.890
been explored in a
couple of studies

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:42.580
is 10 to 20 milligrams per day.

00:25:42.580 --> 00:25:44.730
Again, you could
get that from foods.

00:25:44.730 --> 00:25:48.690
You could go above that
if you're more cavalier,

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:51.330
or you could go toward
the lower end of that,

00:25:51.330 --> 00:25:55.860
10 to 20 milligrams per
day, if you are somebody

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:56.980
who's more conservative.

00:25:56.980 --> 00:25:57.760
I should say.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:26:00.262
Well, there aren't
people out there who are,

00:26:00.262 --> 00:26:02.220
looking at lutein, they
think it's fairly safe,

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:04.053
and they're taking
higher dosages of lutein,

00:26:04.053 --> 00:26:05.460
so 20 to 30 milligrams per day.

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:07.170
They're also taking
supplements that

00:26:07.170 --> 00:26:09.720
contain zeaxanthins
and astaxanthins

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:13.350
and are reporting anecdotally
that their vision is enhanced.

00:26:13.350 --> 00:26:18.060
I don't dispute those
claims, but as of now, there

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:21.660
aren't many papers, I
could only find about 4,

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:25.860
3 really solid ones and
then a fourth, that point

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:27.900
to the specific
disease instances

00:26:27.900 --> 00:26:31.350
where supplementing with lutein,
zeaxanthin, or astaxanthin

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:33.840
can be beneficial for slowing
the progression of things

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:37.020
like advanced onset age-related
macular degeneration.

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:40.560
And the degree of offset for
that macular degeneration

00:26:40.560 --> 00:26:41.100
was slight.

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:44.610
It wasn't enormous, but
it was robust enough

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:47.280
that it warranted
reporting in the paper.

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:49.800
Or things like Lieber's, which
is a particular eye disease.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:51.960
It impacts a whole different
set of systems in the eye.

00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:53.130
So basically, what
I'm trying to say

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:55.020
is, when it comes to supplements
to support eye health,

00:26:55.020 --> 00:26:57.395
make sure you're getting enough
vitamin A from your diet.

00:26:57.395 --> 00:26:59.370
If you're not, consider
supplementation

00:26:59.370 --> 00:27:01.230
or consider changing
your diet somewhat

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:04.020
to make sure you can get
that lutein and vitamin A.

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:07.020
And if you are interested in
it, and it's within your budget,

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:11.100
I don't know of any
evidence that supplementing

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:14.010
with additional lutein,
zeaxanthin, and astaxanthin

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:15.160
can be dangerous at all.

00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:15.960
So for those of
you that are really

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:18.232
interested in maintaining
or improving your vision,

00:27:18.232 --> 00:27:20.190
that might be an area
that you want to explore.

00:27:20.190 --> 00:27:23.160
I personally am not
taking lutein, zeaxanthin,

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:25.830
or astaxanthin, at
least not at this time.

00:27:25.830 --> 00:27:28.260
I do take a
foundational supplement,

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:32.800
which is my source of vitamin A
in addition to my food sources.

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:34.580
But I don't suffer
from vision loss

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:37.290
anywhere, readers at night,
and I have for a long time.

00:27:37.290 --> 00:27:40.140
I have a little bit
of a muscular weakness

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:43.578
in one of my eyes that
originated in childhood.

00:27:43.578 --> 00:27:45.870
So occasionally, if I get
very fatigued, one of my eyes

00:27:45.870 --> 00:27:47.495
will deviate a little
bit, a little bit

00:27:47.495 --> 00:27:50.670
of almost strabismus,
especially if I've been awake

00:27:50.670 --> 00:27:52.200
for very long periods of time.

00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:54.665
It tends to recover once I
get a good night's sleep.

00:27:54.665 --> 00:27:56.040
Some of you that
have experienced

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:56.940
this, where you
have one eye that

00:27:56.940 --> 00:27:58.020
might just drift a little bit.

00:27:58.020 --> 00:27:59.430
I could do this for
the camera if you want.

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:00.360
OK, this is weird.

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:03.443
I used to do this to my
sister across the table when

00:28:03.443 --> 00:28:05.110
we were at a friend's
houses for dinner.

00:28:05.110 --> 00:28:06.420
So I can actually
move one of my eyes

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:08.280
in like this, which
actually just reflects

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:12.000
that there's a extraocular
muscle closer to my nose.

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:13.890
It's rather weak, which
allows me to do that.

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:16.140
I can't do it so well
with the opposite eye.

00:28:16.140 --> 00:28:18.180
Anyway, now I need to
blink to clear my vision.

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:20.400
But the point is that,
if you're somebody

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:24.480
who has a slightly
deviating eye, slightly,

00:28:24.480 --> 00:28:26.940
often if you go to the
ophthalmologist or optometrist,

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:28.773
they're going to give
you a prism lens which

00:28:28.773 --> 00:28:31.650
is going to redirect the
image onto that slightly

00:28:31.650 --> 00:28:32.965
deflected eyeball.

00:28:32.965 --> 00:28:34.590
One thing you'll
really want to discuss

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:38.760
with that ophthalmologist or
optometrist is whether or not

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:41.280
there are exercises that
you could do to overcome

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:44.580
that issue without the need
for a prism because what I saw

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:47.790
and what many other people see
when they put on a prism lens

00:28:47.790 --> 00:28:50.370
is that indeed it can
line up their vision

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:52.080
or their visual
perception better,

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:54.840
but indeed, it can lead
to progressive weakening

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:56.830
of the muscle
further and further.

00:28:56.830 --> 00:28:59.400
So then that eyeball starts to
deviate further and further.

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:01.590
And that raises a larger
question, which is,

00:29:01.590 --> 00:29:03.480
should we wear
corrective lenses?

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:04.860
In fact, if you
go online, you'll

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:06.480
find or hear about
these examples

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:08.820
of people that stopped wearing
their corrective lenses,

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:10.650
their glasses or
contacts, in order

00:29:10.650 --> 00:29:12.750
to bring their vision
back or improve

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:14.107
their vision on their own.

00:29:14.107 --> 00:29:15.690
Now, if your vision
is quite impaired,

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:17.842
you're going to need
corrective lenses or contacts.

00:29:17.842 --> 00:29:19.800
However, there are a
number of people out there

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:23.340
who find that if their
vision is somewhat impaired,

00:29:23.340 --> 00:29:26.220
that forcing themselves to
view things at a distance

00:29:26.220 --> 00:29:28.950
to do smooth pursuit,
to do near far,

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:31.590
and to make sure that they're
getting adequate nutrition

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:34.470
and things of that
sort, can all sum

00:29:34.470 --> 00:29:37.530
to help offset some of the
visual impairment that would

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:40.840
occur if they were to just
pop glasses on their face

00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:43.050
and rely on the
corrective lenses

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:46.920
in order to focus the light onto
the correct area within the eye

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:48.060
onto the neural retina.

00:29:48.060 --> 00:29:50.640
So I'm not telling people
to throw off or get rid

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:52.740
of their corrective
lenses and contacts,

00:29:52.740 --> 00:29:56.310
but you can be overly reliant
on these things, like a crutch,

00:29:56.310 --> 00:29:58.735
in a way that perhaps you want
to do a little bit of work

00:29:58.735 --> 00:30:00.360
to try and overcome
and then maybe work

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:01.500
with a weaker prescription.

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:04.080
And certainly, in the
case of prism lenses,

00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:06.117
what I mentioned before
is a real consideration.

00:30:06.117 --> 00:30:07.450
It's one I'm very familiar with.

00:30:07.450 --> 00:30:09.570
So as a last but I
think very important

00:30:09.570 --> 00:30:13.785
and exciting feature of visual
improvement is red light.

00:30:13.785 --> 00:30:16.410
I talked a little bit about this
on the episode of the Huberman

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:18.510
Lab podcast all about
light and health.

00:30:18.510 --> 00:30:20.340
There's some really
spectacular, I

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:23.070
think I consider it spectacular,
many other people consider

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:25.890
it spectacular, research out
of University College London.

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:28.350
And the work I'm referring
to is Glenn Jeffrey's work.

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:29.730
I've known Glenn
for a long time.

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:32.550
These are animal studies,
and now two human studies,

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:37.890
exploring how exposure to
red light early in the day

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:40.680
can offset some of the
vision loss related

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:43.590
to age-related macular
degeneration, or simply

00:30:43.590 --> 00:30:45.378
age-related visual decline.

00:30:45.378 --> 00:30:46.920
There's a whole
mechanism here that's

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:49.295
discussed in that episode,
but it has to do with the fact

00:30:49.295 --> 00:30:53.700
that red light and near-infrared
light, together, or maybe

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:56.250
even separately, when viewed
for even just a minute

00:30:56.250 --> 00:31:00.210
to 3 minutes once, or maybe
two or three times per week

00:31:00.210 --> 00:31:04.230
maximum, can actually
reduce the number of what

00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:06.570
are called reactive oxygen
species in the photoreceptors

00:31:06.570 --> 00:31:08.340
that convert photons
into electrical signals

00:31:08.340 --> 00:31:09.810
the rest of the visual
system can understand.

00:31:09.810 --> 00:31:10.840
So that's a mouthful.

00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:16.600
Basically what they found was,
for people 40 years or older,

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:20.417
if they viewed this red
light at a distance of about

00:31:20.417 --> 00:31:22.000
a foot and a half,
although I wouldn't

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:26.590
get too specific with this, for
a few minutes each week total,

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:28.720
they were able to offset
some of the vision loss

00:31:28.720 --> 00:31:31.237
within a particular
domain of vision.

00:31:31.237 --> 00:31:33.070
And I don't have time
to go into the details

00:31:33.070 --> 00:31:35.770
here because I do that in
the Light for Health episode,

00:31:35.770 --> 00:31:38.690
but you have different types
of photoreceptors, cones,

00:31:38.690 --> 00:31:39.190
and rods.

00:31:39.190 --> 00:31:41.080
You have different types
of cone photoreceptors

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:43.030
that respond to different
wavelengths of light,

00:31:43.030 --> 00:31:44.863
short, medium, and long
wavelengths of light

00:31:44.863 --> 00:31:48.160
so-called blue,
green, and red cones,

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:49.660
they're sometimes called.

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:52.000
When these people who
are 40 years or older

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:55.000
viewed red light on a consistent
basis early in the day,

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:56.800
and it did have to
be early in the day,

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:58.660
there was a real
circadian effect there,

00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:03.120
there was a offset of
some of the vision loss

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:06.180
that would normally occur
in a particular wavelength

00:32:06.180 --> 00:32:10.660
of light range that resulted in
better visual acuity overall.

00:32:10.660 --> 00:32:12.660
So if you're very concerned
about your vision,

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:15.330
or you're into red light
therapies, et cetera,

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:18.630
there are a number of different
red light devices out there.

00:32:18.630 --> 00:32:20.880
At some future point,
the Huberman Lab podcast

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:23.297
may partner with one of
these red light companies.

00:32:23.297 --> 00:32:25.380
So right now, I'm not going
to mention any of them

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:25.770
in particular.

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:26.970
They're prominent online.

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:29.850
What you're looking for is a
red light unit that provides

00:32:29.850 --> 00:32:31.350
red light and maybe far--

00:32:31.350 --> 00:32:34.120
near infrared-- excuse
me-- light as well.

00:32:34.120 --> 00:32:40.180
So something in the 650 to
720 ish nanometer range.

00:32:40.180 --> 00:32:42.510
So really it's going to
look red or almost invisible

00:32:42.510 --> 00:32:45.420
to you if it's really
far into the infrared.

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:48.603
And you'll want to view
that at a distance of about

00:32:48.603 --> 00:32:49.770
a foot and a half to 2 feet.

00:32:49.770 --> 00:32:50.940
And you don't want
it to be so bright

00:32:50.940 --> 00:32:52.380
that it's painful to
look at because you

00:32:52.380 --> 00:32:53.470
don't want to damage your eyes.

00:32:53.470 --> 00:32:55.735
But that's essentially what
they used in these studies

00:32:55.735 --> 00:32:56.610
from the Jeffrey lab.

00:32:56.610 --> 00:32:58.320
And they showed some
pretty impressive

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:01.290
offset of some of the
aspects of vision loss

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:02.530
in these people 40 and older.

00:33:02.530 --> 00:33:04.110
So that's an area
that you may want

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:07.380
to explore as well, especially
for folks 40 and older.

00:33:07.380 --> 00:33:09.150
And, again, this
red light exposure

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:10.500
had to be done early in the day.

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:13.650
So hopefully, that collection
of behavioral tools near, far,

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:16.350
smooth pursuit, some of the
discussion about lutein,

00:33:16.350 --> 00:33:19.140
nutrition, vitamin A,
supplementation, zeaxanthins,

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:22.980
astaxanthins, getting two
hours of light exposure

00:33:22.980 --> 00:33:25.350
outside, sunlight exposure,
get outside to do some

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:29.520
of your work, and perhaps even
deliberate red light exposure

